 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Complexity:** Time complexity grows exponentially with the number of bounces and optical thickness of the medium [S2, unofficial]. Memory complexity depends on the volume grid resolution and storage format (e.g., OpenVDB) [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Rendering dense clouds with multiple scattering is significantly slower than single scattering [S2, unofficial]. GPU rendering and approximations are often required to manage costs [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Produces realistic brightness and volume for dense media like cumulus clouds (which can reflect up to 90% of incident light) [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** High noise levels; limited by practical render times; not suitable for real-time without approximation [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** For offline rendering of dense clouds, smoke, or subsurface scattering where visual fidelity is critical [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** For thin media like light haze (single scattering suffices) or real-time applications requiring low latency [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The cost increases exponentially with the `Volume Trace Depth` because every added bounce multiplies the work [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the volume data structure (e.g., OpenVDB) and the recursion depth of the ray tracer [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Produces realistic brightness for dense media like clouds and smoke, which appear dark and lifeless under single scattering [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Computationally expensive; unbiased rendering is noisy and slow [S1]. For materials with well-defined surfaces like skin or milk, subsurface scattering is more efficient and easier to control [S1].
+*   **When to use:** For media without well-defined surfaces, such as clouds or smoke, where many scattering bounces are required for visual fidelity [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** For thin media like light haze, where single scattering is sufficient [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Multiple scattering is a fundamental concept in radiative transfer theory, essential for simulating real-world effects like scattering in clouds and subsurface scattering [S1]. In computer graphics, it has been a challenge for unbiased renderers due to its computational cost and noise. Blender's Cycles renderer, for instance, defaults to zero volume bounces (single scattering) due to noise concerns, while Eevee does not support it [S1]. The industry has adopted OpenVDB for volume data exchange, originally developed at DreamWorks Animation and open-sourced in 2012 [S2, unofficial].
+Multiple scattering is a fundamental concept in radiative transfer, essential for simulating real-world effects like scattering in clouds or subsurface scattering [S1]. In computer graphics, it has historically been challenging to implement efficiently due to the computational cost of tracing multiple bounces [S2, unofficial]. Modern renderers often limit the number of bounces by default (e.g., zero in Cycles) to maintain performance, requiring explicit enabling for multiple scattering [S1]. The industry has adopted formats like OpenVDB for transporting volume data, originally developed at DreamWorks Animation and open-sourced in 2012, to support these complex simulations [S2, unofficial].
 
